\subsection{Reduced drift--distortion optimum}
\label{sec:riccati-mis-specified-pseudo}

The Riccati analysis quantifies covariance contraction but not distortion of
real wave motion, for which $S$ is generally nonzero.  A reduced asymptotic cost
adds this missing term.

Below the posterior acceleration correlation rate, approximate residual
acceleration error by white noise of zero-frequency intensity
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and use the continuous-equivalent $S$-correction bandwidth
$\omega_R$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:riccati-omegaR}.  For a wave spectrum sufficiently
separated above this bandwidth, the leading position mean-square error is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
J_{\mathrm{red}}(\omega_R)
\simeq
\frac{3q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{2\omega_R^3}
+4m_{-4}\omega_R^4,}
\qquad
m_{-4}:=\int_{\Omega_{\mathrm{wave}}}
\frac{S_p(\omega)}{\omega^4}\,d\omega .
\label{eq:reduced-mse-tradeoff}
\end{equation}
The terms represent residual-integrator drift and rejection of desired wave
motion, respectively.  The minimizer is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
(\omega_R^{\star})^7
=\frac{9}{32}\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{m_{-4}},}
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-omega}
\end{equation}
and, with $R_S=r_S^2$,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{red}}^{\star}
=\left(\frac{32}{9}\right)^{3/7}
q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 m_{-4}^{3/7}T_S^{-1/2}.}
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}
\end{equation}
At this point,
\begin{equation}
J_{\mathrm{red}}^{\star}
\propto q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{4/7}m_{-4}^{3/7}.
\label{eq:reduced-optimal-mse-scaling}
\end{equation}
Thus drift intensity changes the preferred regularizer only as
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}$ but changes the minimized residual error as
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{4/7}$; a dominant disturbance need not imply a comparably
large change in useful regularization strength.

Equations~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-omega}--\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}
exactly minimize the stated asymptotic cost, not the complete 21-state MEKF.
Attitude/bias and three-axis covariance coupling, discrete pseudo-updates,
endogenous $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$, and imperfect spectral separation limit the
reduction, particularly in changing or multimodal seas.  The result is therefore
used for mechanism and sensitivity rather than as a closed-form production
optimum.
